  {"id": "F0807-29", "bucket": "04-light-dark-placement", "lane": "reference", "territory": "printmaking-remix", "class": "reference", "score": 6, "wildcard": false, "title": "Dragon and Tiger — six-panel ink screen", "brand": "Kano school, Japan, c. 1546–56 — Cleveland Museum of Art, CC0", "note": "LENS: mythology. The most extreme NEGATIVE SPACE example in the system — roughly two thirds of the surface is bare cream paper doing nothing, and that is why the dragon lands. Mechanics: 1) the dragon is only PARTIALLY RESOLVED — head, claw and a coil of body emerge from ink-wash cloud and the rest dissolves back into it, so the creature and the atmosphere are the same substance; 2) the tiger crouches low-left in rock while the dragon occupies the upper air — a diagonal tension across the whole width; 3) a huge wash SPIRAL on the right is almost empty, a loud element made of nothing. Monochrome: warm cream ground, grey-black ink, zero colour. Directly buildable as front-quiet/back-loud on a torso.", "img": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1959.136.1/1959.136.1_web.jpg", "source": "https://clevelandart.org/art/1959.136.1", "tags": ["negative space", "ink wash", "partially resolved figure", "monochrome", "diagonal tension"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-30", "bucket": "08-standout-graphic", "lane": "reference", "territory": "neo-japanese", "class": "reference", "score": 6, "wildcard": false, "title": "Cut-gold phoenix ornament", "brand": "China, Tang dynasty, c. 700s — Cleveland Museum of Art, CC0", "note": "LENS: craft and material. A creature reduced to ONE FLAT GOLD SILHOUETTE with almost no interior detail. The mechanic worth taking: the extremities stop being anatomy and BECOME ORNAMENT — the raised wing resolves into a flame-like frond, the tail into curling tendrils, so the outline does all the work. Single colour, gold on near-black. This is gold as FILL, where Ladana's UFC 288 keep was gold as LINE — worth holding the two next to each other, because they are the two different ways to spend one metallic on a black base.", "img": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1942.1083.2/1942.1083.2_web.jpg", "source": "https://clevelandart.org/art/1942.1083.2", "tags": ["gold on black", "flat silhouette", "extremities become ornament", "single colour"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-31", "bucket": "08-standout-graphic", "lane": "reference", "territory": "luxury-streetwear", "class": "reference", "score": 6, "wildcard": false, "title": "Scythian stag plaque — antler as rhythm", "brand": "Western Asia, Scythian, 400–300 BCE — Cleveland Museum of Art, CC0", "note": "LENS: ancient and archaeological. Two mechanics. 1) The animal is COMPRESSED INTO A CLOSED, NEARLY CIRCULAR SILHOUETTE — legs folded under, neck arched back — so it reads as a medallion rather than as a running animal. That is a chest-roundel solution 2,400 years old. 2) The antler is not drawn as an antler: it is abstracted into a ROW OF REPEATING HOOKS running the whole length of the spine, turning one anatomical feature into a rhythmic repeat. Take the second mechanic and apply it to any creature — the repeat is what makes it graphic instead of illustrative.", "img": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1985.77/1985.77_web.jpg", "source": "https://clevelandart.org/art/1985.77", "tags": ["closed silhouette", "roundel", "anatomy as repeat", "gold", "ancient"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-32", "bucket": "01-colour-palette", "lane": "reference", "territory": "luxury-streetwear", "class": "reference", "score": 6, "wildcard": false, "title": "Feathered panel — blue and yellow, 2×2", "brand": "Peru, Pampa Ocoña, 600–900 CE — Cleveland Museum of Art, CC0", "note": "LENS: cultural symbol systems. THIS IS THE ANSWER TO THE TATAMI CONTRAST REJECT. Same structure Ladana cut as plain — hard-edged two-colour blocking — but here it is premium, and the difference is one thing: SURFACE. The blocks are thousands of macaw feathers, so each colour field has directional pile, catches light unevenly and carries tonal variation inside a single colour. Deep ultramarine with darker flecks against a warm saturated gold with orange drift. Hard edges, no blending, two colours only. The lesson: colour blocking fails when the colour is FLAT, not because it is blocking. Give a block a texture and it becomes a material.", "img": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2002.93/2002.93_web.jpg", "source": "https://clevelandart.org/art/2002.93", "tags": ["two-colour block", "texture rescues blocking", "ultramarine and gold", "directional pile"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-33", "bucket": "06-garment-layout", "lane": "reference", "territory": "luxury-streetwear", "class": "reference", "score": 6, "wildcard": false, "title": "Javanese sarong — three zones, hard vertical seams", "brand": "Java, North Coast, 1800s — Cleveland Museum of Art, CC0", "note": "LENS: cultural symbol systems. A GARMENT LAYOUT find, not a pattern find. One cloth is divided into three completely different fields by hard vertical borders: a dark indigo band with a row of white saw-tooth points aiming inward, then a narrow ruled divider, then a warm ochre field of dense small motifs, then a cool bone-grey field with a different motif entirely. Colour AND pattern change at the seam, with no blending. This is the kepala/badan structure of a sarong and it is a real alternative to the single all-over print: zones with abrupt transitions, one of them deliberately quiet.", "img": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1928.656/1928.656_web.jpg", "source": "https://clevelandart.org/art/1928.656", "tags": ["vertical zones", "abrupt transition", "tumpal sawtooth", "indigo ochre bone", "garment layout"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-34", "bucket": "03-pattern", "lane": "reference", "territory": "luxury-streetwear", "class": "reference", "score": 5, "wildcard": false, "title": "Kuba raffia pile cloth — imperfect interlace", "brand": "Kuba peoples, DR Congo, 1900s — Cleveland Museum of Art, CC0", "note": "LENS: cultural symbol systems. The counter-example to every GENERIC geometric reject on this board. It is a maze/meander interlace in dark brown on natural tan — the same family as the stock linework Ladana cut — but it reads as crafted for one reason: IT IS DELIBERATELY IRREGULAR. Line widths shift, blocks fail to align, and the rhythm changes across the cloth without ever repeating exactly. Cut-pile gives it a velvet surface. Two tones only. If FK ever builds a geometric all-over, this is the reference: hand-drawn variance is what separates a pattern from a texture map.", "img": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1992.102/1992.102_web.jpg", "source": "https://clevelandart.org/art/1992.102", "tags": ["irregular geometry", "interlace", "two-tone", "cut pile", "hand-drawn variance"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-35", "bucket": "02-font-type", "lane": "reference", "territory": "editorial-composition", "class": "reference", "score": 6, "wildcard": false, "title": "Chinese semi-cursive calligraphy — one character against many", "brand": "Qing dynasty, c. 1660–1709 — Cleveland Museum of Art, CC0", "note": "LENS: calligraphy. The clearest EXTREME TYPE-SCALE CONTRAST reference found so far, and the same mechanic as the Serpent benchmark. One enormous black character sits at the right, perhaps five times the size of everything else, laid down in a single loaded stroke with visible DRY-BRUSH SPLIT at the edges where the ink ran out. The rest is a column grid of small cursive characters. Three red seals — two clustered low-left, one top-right — are the ONLY colour on the sheet. Warm cream ground. Take: one giant gesture, a field of small type, and a single saturated accent used three times.", "img": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2003.353/2003.353_web.jpg", "source": "https://clevelandart.org/art/2003.353", "tags": ["type scale contrast", "dry brush", "red seal accent", "column grid", "one gesture"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-36", "bucket": "04-light-dark-placement", "lane": "reference", "territory": "luxury-streetwear", "class": "reference", "score": 5, "wildcard": false, "title": "Indigo sarong — the same motif positive and negative", "brand": "Java, Indramayu, early 1900s — Cleveland Museum of Art, CC0", "note": "LENS: cultural symbol systems. A second Javanese sarong doing the vertical split differently, which is what makes it worth filing next to F0807-33 — two independent examples means this is a TRADITION, not a one-off. Here the split is TONAL INVERSION: the left third is deep indigo with the floral motif reversed out in white, the right two thirds is cream with the same motif family drawn in indigo. One drawing, run light-on-dark then dark-on-light, meeting at a hard vertical edge. A scalloped border runs the hem. This is the cheapest possible way to make front and back read as different garments while staying one design.", "img": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1917.27/1917.27_web.jpg", "source": "https://clevelandart.org/art/1917.27", "tags": ["tonal inversion", "vertical split", "indigo", "one motif two ways", "scalloped border"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-37", "bucket": "08-standout-graphic", "lane": "combat", "territory": "neo-japanese", "class": "buildable", "score": 6, "wildcard": false, "title": "UFC Unrivaled — Alexandre Pantoja fight short", "brand": "Venum × UFC, published 2026-07-16", "note": "LENS: fightwear. The strongest product find of the run and it converges with Ladana's own keeps. Black base, deep red-to-orange gradient panels, and a DRAGON DRAWN ENTIRELY IN GOLD LINE coiled across the hip — gold as a contour, never as a fill, which is exactly the mechanic she kept from UFC 288. Around it: a chevron ray pattern radiating from the hip, Japanese cloud scroll at the waistband, and PANTOJA set in gold running a long diagonal. Three mechanics stacked — gold line, radiating chevrons, diagonal name — on a black ground with one hot colour family. Note it is a SHORT: the mechanics transfer to a torso, the layout does not.", "img": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0767/3072/8767/files/d6dc108cc2f495a14b33db592c3133829a9bc6ed_VNMUFC_00553_527_G_1.jpg", "source": "https://row.venum.com/products/men-s-ufc-unrivaled-by-venum-red-alexandre-pantoja-vale-tudo-short", "tags": ["gold as line", "dragon", "radiating chevrons", "diagonal name", "black base"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-38", "bucket": "04-light-dark-placement", "lane": "combat", "territory": "cyberpunk-tech", "class": "buildable", "score": 5, "wildcard": false, "title": "UFC Zenith Fight Week rashguard — white ground", "brand": "Venum × UFC, published 2026-07-16", "note": "LENS: fightwear. Filed mainly because it is the OPPOSITE of the FK catalogue: a LIGHT-GROUND rash guard, where all 208 FK rash guards anchor on black. The graphic is black camo built from torn, photocopy-degraded fragments — hard-edged shapes with fine horizontal line-screen inside some of them, so it reads as a generation-loss artefact rather than as camouflage. Small hot orange marks appear maybe four times across the whole garment and are the only colour. Worth a decision rather than a glance: is a white-ground FK piece interesting, or off-brand? The board has no other evidence either way.", "img": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0767/3072/8767/files/dbc179a445d04659dee6f0caeacef34b737dc15f_VNMUFC_00430_108_G_01.jpg", "source": "https://row.venum.com/products/mens-ufc-zenith-by-venum-white-authentic-fight-week-performance-long-sleeve-rashguard", "tags": ["light ground", "generation loss", "line screen", "sparse hot accent", "inverted for FK"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-39", "bucket": "03-pattern", "lane": "combat", "territory": "luxury-streetwear", "class": "buildable", "score": 4, "wildcard": false, "title": "UFC Unrivaled — Alex Pereira vale tudo short", "brand": "Venum × UFC, published 2026-07-16", "note": "LENS: fightwear, crossed with natural science. THIRD animal-hide data point after the croc shorts and the Matupa snake, and the one that changes the rule: the leopard rosettes are RECOLOURED TO HOT RED, not left in natural tan. Hide print does not have to be naturalistic — the structure of the rosette survives any colour. Set against panels of bright folk-geometric triangles in blue, yellow and green. Honest score of 4: the two systems fight each other and there is no quiet zone anywhere. Filed for the recolouring idea, not for the layout.", "img": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0767/3072/8767/files/85a1c7f9ba4ecfc38903ad4a46fa43fcafbe77b0_VNMUFC_00548_609_G_1.jpg", "source": "https://row.venum.com/products/men-s-ufc-unrivaled-by-red-venum-alex-pereira-second-edition-vale-tudo-short", "tags": ["recoloured hide print", "leopard rosette", "folk geometry", "too busy", "hot red"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-40", "bucket": "05-mood", "lane": "combat", "territory": "luxury-streetwear", "class": "buildable", "score": 4, "wildcard": false, "title": "Rage & Workout long sleeve rashguard — black/grey", "brand": "Venum, published 2026-08-05 — newest drop found this run", "note": "LENS: fightwear. The newest Venum drop at time of run, filed for trend-tracking as much as for design. All-over mottled black-and-grey that reads like weathered stone or a dilute ink wash, with NO hero graphic at all — the texture is the entire design. Directly relevant to the HIDE concept, which proposes exactly this. Honest score of 4 and a warning: Ladana cut the Venum Stealth for being plain, and this is quieter still. The open question it puts on the table is whether an all-texture garment reads as premium restraint or as an empty shirt — worth a verdict, because HIDE depends on the answer.", "img": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0767/3072/8767/files/7425f26d3c05f96a07e01b93cf33106d41f3e830_VENUM_06384_109_G_1.jpg", "source": "https://row.venum.com/products/venum-rage-workout-long-sleeve-rashguard-black-grey", "tags": ["texture as whole design", "no hero", "tonal mottling", "tests the HIDE concept"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-41", "bucket": "02-font-type", "lane": "combat", "territory": "editorial-composition", "class": "buildable", "score": 5, "wildcard": false, "title": "8 Weapons — 8 Ways Yant 2.0, black/bordeaux", "brand": "8 Weapons via Khun Pon, €54.90", "note": "LENS: tattoo culture crossed with mythology — the crossing suggested last run, and it exists as product already. Black torso, bordeaux raglan sleeves, split cleanly at the raglan seam. The chest carries a sak yant design: concentric ARCS OF SCRIPT in red and white curving around a central white brush wordmark, with small rectangular glyph blocks repeating along the arcs. The script follows the chest in a horseshoe rather than sitting square, so the type wraps the body. Restrained four-value palette. ⚠️ CULTURAL CAUTION: sak yant are sacred Thai Buddhist tattoos, traditionally applied and blessed by monks. Take the FORMAL mechanic — script arranged radially as ornament around a centre — and never the glyphs.", "img": "https://www.khunpon.de/media/9c/21/40/1780040930/8weapons-rashguard-ss-8-ways-yant-2-0-black-red-jpg.jpg", "source": "https://www.khunpon.de/en/clothing/fitness/compression-shirts/8-weapons-rashguard-s-s-8-ways-yant-2.0-black-bordeaux", "tags": ["radial script", "raglan colour split", "sak yant", "cultural caution", "bordeaux"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-42", "bucket": "08-standout-graphic", "lane": "combat", "territory": "luxury-streetwear", "class": "buildable", "score": 5, "wildcard": false, "title": "Pit Bull West Coast — Blacky", "brand": "Pit Bull West Coast via Khun Pon, €44.75", "note": "LENS: fightwear. The TECHNIQUE is the find, not the subject. A dog head fills the entire front torso, rendered in fine WHITE LINE ENGRAVING on black — scraperboard or banknote logic, where tone is built from line density alone and there is not a single grey in it. That is why it survives: sublimation handles pure black and pure white far better than soft mid-tones, so an engraved rendering keeps photographic detail that a greyscale photo would lose. Sleeves left completely empty; all the event is on the torso. One hero, hard negative space, two colours.", "img": "https://www.khunpon.de/media/9f/ef/51/1781688590/pit-bull-west-coast-rashguard-s-s-blacky-schwarz.jpg", "source": "https://www.khunpon.de/en/clothing/fitness/compression-shirts/pit-bull-west-coast-rashguard-s-s-blacky-black", "tags": ["line engraving", "no mid-tones", "one hero", "empty sleeves", "survives sublimation"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-43", "bucket": "06-garment-layout", "lane": "combat", "territory": "motorsport-graphics", "class": "buildable", "score": 4, "wildcard": false, "title": "OKAMI — Team Germany", "brand": "OKAMI via Khun Pon, €49.90", "note": "LENS: cultural symbol systems, applied commercially. A COUNTRY EDITION that avoids looking like a flag, which is the hard part of the idea in SEEDS S19. The torso stays black with a black-on-black heraldic eagle — tonal, almost invisible until the light catches it. The national colours are exiled to the SLEEVES AND HEM ONLY, and they arrive as torn painterly bands rather than clean stripes, so they read as paint rather than as vexillology. Take the structure: quiet tonal torso, national colour as ragged trim. Score 4 because the TEAM GERMANY lockup itself is generic sports italic and undoes some of the restraint.", "img": "https://www.khunpon.de/media/48/d0/3b/1779866501/okami-rashguard-s-s-team-germany-schwarz-jpg.jpg", "source": "https://www.khunpon.de/en/clothing/fitness/compression-shirts/okami-rashguard-s-s-team-germany-black-red-gold", "tags": ["country edition", "black on black", "colour at trim only", "torn bands", "weak lockup"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-44", "bucket": "08-standout-graphic", "lane": "reference", "territory": "printmaking-remix", "class": "reference", "score": 6, "wildcard": false, "title": "Frog — Chōjū-jinbutsu-giga (Frolicking Animals)", "brand": "Attributed Toba Sōjō, Japan, 12th century — public domain via Wikimedia Commons", "note": "LENS: gaming/anime crossed with ancient — and the crossing turns out to be literal. The Chōjū-giga scrolls are the acknowledged ANCESTOR OF MANGA AND ANIME, drawn in the 12th century. The frog is built from perhaps twelve brushstrokes and nothing else: no outline-then-fill, no shading, no colour. The line weight changes WITHIN each stroke as the brush loads and dries, and that variation alone carries the anatomy and the motion. The lesson for a rash guard is economy — a character can be established with a dozen marks, which leaves the rest of the garment empty on purpose.", "img": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Frog%20in%20Choju-giga.jpg?width=1200", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frog_in_Choju-giga.jpg", "tags": ["ancestor of manga", "minimum strokes", "modulated line", "no fill", "economy"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-45", "bucket": "01-colour-palette", "lane": "reference", "territory": "motorsport-graphics", "class": "reference", "score": 6, "wildcard": false, "title": "McLaren MCL60 — Stealth Mode livery", "brand": "Formula One, 2023 — Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0", "note": "LENS: motorsport and technical. The gold-as-line mechanic Ladana already kept, executed in fluorescent orange on matte black — which proves the mechanic is about PLACEMENT, not about the metal. The papaya is confined almost entirely to LEADING EDGES: the nose tip, the front wing endplate, the sidepod inlet lip, the wheel rim. Colour is used to describe where the form turns, so the eye reads the shape of the car from the highlights alone while the body stays black. On a torso that translates to accent along the shoulder seam, the collar and the cuff, with the panels left dark. Sponsor wordmarks sit flat and white on the black.", "img": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/MCL60%20Stealth%20Mode%20Livery.jpg?width=1200", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MCL60_Stealth_Mode_Livery.jpg", "tags": ["accent on leading edges", "matte black", "fluoro orange", "form described by highlight", "stealth"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-46", "bucket": "09-gradient-shape", "lane": "reference", "territory": "editorial-composition", "class": "reference", "score": 5, "wildcard": false, "title": "Risograph print — misregistered spot colour", "brand": "CC0 via Wikimedia Commons", "note": "LENS: contemporary graphic design. A worked example of premium test 4 — print artefact as the thing that stops a design looking cheap. Risograph lays down one spot colour at a time through a soy-ink drum, and it never registers perfectly. Here a hot magenta field sits over a pale blue-grey underlayer, with coarse black speckle breaking across both; the misalignment leaves colour fringes at every edge and the grain is irregular rather than a uniform screen. Two or three flat colours end up reading as a rich, degraded surface. Sublimation can imitate this deliberately: build the artwork in separations, offset them by a few millimetres, add real grain.", "img": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Garden%20Party%2C%20close.jpg?width=1200", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Garden_Party,_close.jpg", "tags": ["risograph", "misregistration", "spot colour", "coarse grain", "magenta and grey"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-47", "bucket": "03-pattern", "lane": "reference", "territory": "luxury-streetwear", "class": "reference", "score": 5, "wildcard": false, "title": "Moth wing scales, macro", "brand": "Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0", "note": "LENS: natural science and anatomy — the micro answer to the croc hide Ladana kept twice. A moth wing is built from thousands of flat scales OVERLAPPING LIKE ROOF SHINGLES, each one catching light at a slightly different angle, so a single warm sepia-brown reads as dozens of values. Two things transfer. First, the shingle structure: overlapping plates in rows is a hide grammar that is not crocodile and not snake, so it is unclaimed. Second, the SHALLOW FOCUS — the pattern is sharp in one band and dissolves either side, which is a way of grading a texture across a body without changing the motif.", "img": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Scales%20of%20a%20moth%20-%20Macro%20photography.jpg?width=1200", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scales_of_a_moth_-_Macro_photography.jpg", "tags": ["overlapping scales", "shingle structure", "one hue many values", "focus falloff", "unclaimed hide"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-48", "bucket": "09-gradient-shape", "lane": "reference", "territory": "cyberpunk-tech", "class": "reference", "score": 5, "wildcard": true, "title": "Bismuth crystal — stepped geometry, iridescent film", "brand": "Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.5", "note": "WILDCARD. LENS: natural science. Filed because nothing on the board looks remotely like it. A bismuth hopper crystal grows as SQUARE TERRACES STEPPING INWARD — hard right-angled geometry that occurs naturally — and the oxide film on top shifts gold to green to blue to pink depending on viewing angle. So it is simultaneously the most rigid geometry and the least controllable colour on the board. The transferable idea: a strictly geometric structure carrying an iridescent gradient that ignores the geometry entirely, one hard system and one soft system on the same surface. Rejecting this costs nothing and narrows nothing.", "img": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bismuth%20crystal%20macro.jpg?width=1200", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bismuth_crystal_macro.jpg", "tags": ["stepped terraces", "iridescence", "hard geometry soft colour", "wildcard"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-49", "bucket": "01-colour-palette", "lane": "reference", "territory": "luxury-streetwear", "class": "reference", "score": 4, "wildcard": true, "title": "Geode — quartz on limonite", "brand": "Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0", "note": "WILDCARD. LENS: natural science and material. A palette and edge study: a rough rust-and-ochre limonite rind wrapping a soft botryoidal interior of pale blue-grey quartz with a trace of teal. Warm rough outside, cool smooth inside, and the boundary between them is RAGGED AND UNREPEATABLE rather than a drawn line. Two ideas: the warm-rust against cool-grey pairing is not in the FK catalogue anywhere, and the torn organic boundary is a better panel-break than a clean curve. Honest score of 4 — it is an ingredient, not a design.", "img": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Geode-with-encrustation-of-quartz-crystals-on-a-base-of-limonite-01.jpg?width=1200", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geode-with-encrustation-of-quartz-crystals-on-a-base-of-limonite-01.jpg", "tags": ["rust and cool grey", "botryoidal", "ragged boundary", "wildcard", "ingredient not design"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-50", "bucket": "03-pattern", "lane": "reference", "territory": "luxury-streetwear", "class": "reference", "score": 4, "wildcard": false, "title": "Sgraffito wall panels, Schwarzenberg Palace, Prague", "brand": "16th century — Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0", "note": "LENS: ancient and craft. A whole facade built from ONE UNIT repeated: a rectangle split on the diagonal into a light half and a dark half, with fine engraved hatching inside each half. Nothing more. Two tones, cream and charcoal. Because it is cut by hand into wet plaster the edges wobble slightly and no two units match exactly, which is what keeps a rigid grid from looking printed. Relevant because the diagonal shoulder-to-hip break is already a known garment mechanic — this shows what happens when you make it the module and tile it, rather than using it once.", "img": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Prague%20Praha%202014%20Holmstad%20sgraffito%20-%20patterns%20at%20Schwarzenberg%20Palace.jpg?width=1200", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prague_Praha_2014_Holmstad_sgraffito_-_patterns_at_Schwarzenberg_Palace.jpg", "tags": ["one unit repeated", "diagonal split", "hand-cut variance", "two tone", "hatching"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"},

  {"id": "F0807-51", "bucket": "01-colour-palette", "lane": "reference", "territory": "luxury-streetwear", "class": "reference", "score": 4, "wildcard": false, "title": "Luna moth wing — jade and amber", "brand": "Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0", "note": "LENS: natural science. Filed purely as a PALETTE, and a quiet one: a soft desaturated jade green wing, a warm amber feathered antenna as the only accent, against near-black foliage. Nothing in the FK catalogue is this muted — the range runs saturated red, blue and black. Ladana kept the Troy Lee dark olive and dusty pink jersey for exactly this kind of unexpected muted pairing, and kept the khaki Stealth colourway for the same reason, so there is now a small pattern of her responding to soft unusual colour rather than to loud colour. Jade with amber on black is the version of that no competitor is running.", "img": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Actias%20luna%20wing%201.jpg?width=1200", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Actias_luna_wing_1.jpg", "tags": ["jade green", "amber accent", "muted palette", "unclaimed colourway"], "firstSeen": "2026-08-07"}
